ckOff, Error, or similar states, that\'s a sign of problems. Also, checking the logs of those pods could help identify specific errors. But first, I need to list all pods to see their status. So the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". That way, I can spot any pods that aren\'t in the Ready state. If there are issues, then I can proceed to check their logs or metrics.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The previous command listed deployments, but to identify faults, I need to check the actual pod statuses. Pods may have errors (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error states) that deployments alone don\'t reveal. Running "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will show pod statuses, including any that are not Ready. This is critical for identifying faulty microservices before checking logs or metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=338, prompt_tokens=2106, total_tokens=2444, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb09-54a5-7ef1-9d53-052016f8e696-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2106, 'output_tokens': 338, 'total_tokens': 2444}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='373ffeb6-2718-48ba-94a7-c77a92538d98')]]
