tion" to check the pod statuses. That makes sense because even if the deployment is healthy, individual pods might have problems like CrashLoopBackOff or other error states. So, the next tool call should be to get the pods.\n\nLooking at the available tools, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd is the right choice. The command should be "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". This will list all pods in the specified namespace, showing their current status. If any pods are not in the "Ready" state, that\'s a sign of a problem. \n\nOnce the pods are listed, the user can identify which ones are faulty. Then, they can proceed to check logs, metrics, or traces for those specific pods. For example, if a pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, they might use kubectl logs to see the error messages. Alternatively, if there are issues with network dependencies, checking the dependency graph or metrics could help pinpoint the root cause.\n\nSo, the next step is to execute the command to get the pods. This will provide the necessary information to determine if any microservices are experiencing issues at the pod level.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-91654b0fb8a19989', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 347, 'prompt_tokens': 3104, 'total_tokens': 3451, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb09-642f-7d43-a8ff-f52250a11718-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-91654b0fb8a19989', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3104, 'output