don\'t indicate exhaustion. The dependency graph didn\'t include the profile service, and there are no traces for it. The endpoints for the services it depends on, like MongoDB and Memcached, seem to be present. \n\nWait, maybe the issue is with the service discovery configuration in the profile service. The logs mentioned connecting to "mongodb-profile:27017" and "memcached-profile:11211". Let me check if the service names are correct. The endpoints show that the MongoDB profile service is named "mongodb-profile" and the Memcached is "memcached-profile". The profile pod\'s logs are trying to connect to those exact service names, which match the endpoints. So that seems correct.\n\nHmm, but maybe there\'s a network policy blocking the communication? Or perhaps the profile service isn\'t properly registered with Consul, which the logs mention. Wait, the logs show that the profile service registered with Consul, and it was successful. So maybe the problem is elsewhere.\n\nWait, the profile pod\'s restarts might be due to a different reason. Let me check the other services that have restarts. The user pod has 2 restarts, rate has 2, and user has 2. Maybe there\'s a common issue affecting multiple services. But the profile pod\'s logs don\'t show any errors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is with the environment variables or configuration files. For example, if the profile service is using a wrong database URL or Memcached address. But the logs show that the database URL is set to "mongodb-profile:27017" and Memcached to "memcached-profile:11211", which are correct based on the endpoints. \n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the profile service is not actually making any requests, so there are no traces. But the logs show that it\'s initializing the database and Memcached clients successfully. Maybe the service is crashing due to a panic or unhandled exception that isn\'t logged. But the logs don\'t show that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod is restarting because of a liveness probe failure. The profile pod might not be passing the liveness probe, causing Kubernetes to restart it. Let me check the pod\'s configuration for liveness and readiness probes. \n\nBut I don\'t have a tool to check the pod\'s YAML configuration. However, I can use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to describe the pod and look for probe configurations. Let me try that. \n\nSo, the next step is to use kubectl describe pod on the profile pod to check for any probe failures or other issues that might cause restarts. That should give more insight into why the pod is restarting despite no obvious errors in the logs.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The "profile" pod has restarts but no clear errors in logs, metrics, or traces. To investigate potential liveness/readiness probe failures or configuration issues, I need to inspect the pod\'s detailed configuration. The "kubectl describe pod" command will reveal probe statuses, container statuses, and any events that might explain the restarts.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 724, 'prompt_tokens': 6932, 'total_tokens': 7656, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0a-bd76-77f2-839a-772dfa28b20f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6932, 'output_tokens': 724, 'total_tokens': 7656}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b6107e8f-275f-4c11-8e77-92e9146d451e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the "profile" pod is restarting. So far, they checked the logs, resource usage, dependency graph, and traces, but nothing obvious showed up. The next step was to look at the pod\'s describe output to check for probe failures or configuration issues.\n\nThe describe pod command gives detailed info about the pod\'s status, events, and container statuses. If there are liveness or readiness probes failing, that could cause the pod to restart. Also, checking the events section might show any errors or issues that occurred before the restarts. Since the pod has restarts but no clear errors in logs, this could be a probe failure or a configuration mistake. Let me check the describe output to find any clues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9d71866320ac1e04', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 204, 'prompt_tokens': 7962, 'total_tokens': 8166, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0a-e06c-7901-bf8e-f2a62dbb5f2d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9d71866320ac1e04', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7962, 'output_tokens': 204, 'total_tokens': 8166}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.32\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 09:01:32 +0000\nLabels: