nic or error. Since the logs don\'t show any errors, maybe the logs are not being captured properly, or the container is exiting before the logs are written. \n\nGiven that the container exits with code 2, which is a generic error, and there\'s no detailed log, the next step would be to check the container\'s logs again, but maybe with more detailed settings. However, the user already checked the logs. \n\nWait, the container\'s image might have a problem. Maybe the image is not compatible with the Kubernetes environment, or there\'s a missing library. The user could try pulling the image again or checking its contents. \n\nAlternatively, the application might be configured to exit after a certain period, but that\'s less likely. \n\nConsidering all this, the most probable cause is an issue with the container image. The image might be incorrect, or the application within it has a bug that causes it to exit with code 2. The user should verify the image\'s correctness and ensure it\'s properly built with all necessary dependencies.\n</think>\n\ntool name: submit_tool  \nreasoning: The "profile" pod is restarting with exit code 2, but no clear errors in logs, metrics, or traces. The container image "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest" might be misconfigured or incompatible, causing the application to fail silently. Since no other faults were detected, the root cause is likely an issue with the container image or its configuration.  \n\nans: The root cause of the failure in the "profile" microservice is an issue with the container image "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest". The pod restarts with exit code 2, indicating an application-level error, but no specific logs or metrics reveal the exact cause. This suggests the image may be misconfigured, missing dependencies, or incompatible with the runtime environment, leading to silent failures. The image should be re-validated or rebuilt to resolve the issue.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': No