**Args**:  
- No arguments required. - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b93cc0c4-67be-4a33-b490-c7d66f2d78ac'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application that has multiple microservices. They\'ve been using various tools to diagnose issues, and the main problem seems to be with the "profile" microservice.\n\nFirst, they checked the deployments and pods, and everything looked okay at first glance. But then they noticed some restarts in the pods, especially the "profile" pod. They looked into the logs, but there weren\'t any obvious errors. The CPU and memory metrics were normal, so resource issues weren\'t the problem. The dependency graph didn\'t show the profile service interacting with others, and the traces for the profile service were empty. \n\nThen, they checked the endpoints for the services the profile depends on, like MongoDB and Memcached, and those were present. When they described the pod, they saw that the container exited with code 2, which is a generic error. The image used was "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest", and it was pulled successfully. \n\nSince there were no specific errors in the logs or metrics, the next step was to consider the container image itself. Exit code 2 can mean different things, but without more info, it\'s hard to tell. The user concluded that the image might be misconfigured or incompatible, leading to silent failures. \n\nSo, the final answer points to the container image as the root cause. The user submitted that the issue is with the image, suggesting it needs re-validation or rebuilding. That makes sense because if the image is broken, the container would fail to start properly, leading to restarts without clear logs. The tools used didn\'t catch the image issue because the logs were silent, so the only clue was the exit code and the restarts.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure in the **"profile" microservice** is an issue with the container image **"yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest"**. The pod restarts with **exit code 2**, indicating an application-level error, but no specific logs or metrics reveal the exact cause. This suggests the image may be **misconfigured**, **missing dependencies**, or **incompatible with the runtime environment**, leading to silent failures. The image should be **re-validated or rebuilt** to resolve the issue. \n\n**Fault Location**:  \n- **Microservice**: `profile`  \n- **Root Cause**: Container image (`yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`) is faulty, causing silent application crashes and restarts.  \n\n**Recommendation**:  \nRebuild or re-validate the container image to ensure compatibility and correctness. Check for missing dependencies or configuration errors in the image.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0f5516ef-d635-4d80-aef9-1624b446a7c9'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b169707f-5afb-4724-a95c-26531dc96fb0'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where the "profile" microservice is having issues. The previous analysis pointed to the container image being faulty, causing silent failures. But I need to make sure that\'s the actual root cause.\n\nFirst, I should check if there are any traces or logs that might give more insight. The get_traces tool can help here. By looking at the Jaeger traces for the "profile" service, I can see if there are any errors or anomalies in the service\'s operations. If the traces are empty, that might confirm that the service isn\'t processing requests, which aligns with the exit code 2 issue.\n\nAlternatively, checking the dependency graph with get_dependency_graph could show if the profile service is interacting with other services incorrectly. But since the user mentioned that the dependency graph didn\'t show interactions, maybe that\'s not the issue. \n\nAnother angle is to check the metrics using get_metrics. If the profile service\'s metrics are abnormal, like high error rates or latency, that could indicate a problem. But since the user already checked CPU and memory metrics and they were normal, maybe that\'s not it. \n\nThe get_logs tool isn\'t listed, but the user mentioned logs didn\'t show errors. However, the exit code 2 is a generic error, so maybe the logs are not detailed enough. \n\nSince the user is considering the container image as the root cause, the next step is to verify if the image is indeed the problem. But without more data, it\'s hard to confirm. The get_traces tool can provide more detailed information about the service\'s operations, which might reveal if there\'s an error in the service\'s processing that\'s not captured elsewhere. \n\nSo, using get_traces for the "profile" service in the last 5 minutes could help identify if there are any traces of errors or if the service is failing silently. If there are no traces, it might confirm that the service isn\'t processing requests, pointing to the image issue. If there are traces, they might show the exact point of failure. \n\nTherefore, the next tool to use is get_traces with the service name "profile" and a reasonable time frame like 5 minutes.\n</think>\n\nget_traces  \n**Reasoning**: To investigate the "profile" microservice\'s behavior, we need to check if there are any traces of errors or anomalies in its operations. The exit code 2 suggests a silent failure, and traces can reveal if the service is crashing, timing out, or encountering unhandled exceptions. This will help confirm if the issue is within the service\'s logic or external dependencies.  \n\n**Args**:  \n- `service`: "profile"  \n- `last_n_minutes`: 5', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 563, 'prompt_tokens': 2232, 'total_tokens': 2795, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0b-5f5c-79e3-ba94-397a7b3eb539-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2232, 'output_tokens': 563, 'total_tokens': 2795}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9941c0de-7946-47c7-8c20-751077758f30'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me think. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" microservice is failing. They mentioned that the pod restarts with exit code 2, but there are no obvious errors in the logs or metrics. The previous step was to call get_traces for the "profile" service over the last 5 minutes. \n\nWait, the tool call needs to be in the correct JSON format. The function is get_traces, and the arguments are service and last_n_minutes. The service name is "profile", and the time frame is 5 minutes. So the tool call should have the name as "get_traces" and the arguments as {"service": "profile", "last_n_minutes": 5}. \n\nI need to make sure that the JSON is properly formatted, with the service name as a string and the minutes as an integer. Also, check if the parameters are required. The get_traces function requires both service and last_n_minutes, which are provided. Alright, that should be correct.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "profile", "last_n_minutes": 5}', 'name': 'get_traces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b9299c9339f32cd5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 243, 'prompt_tokens': 3360, 'total_tokens': 3603, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0b-797d-7090-9535-0e605d34902c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_traces', 'args': {'service': 'profile', 'last_n_minutes': 5}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b9299c9339f32cd5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3360, 'output_tokens': 243, 'total_tokens': 3603}), ToolMessage(content="('[]', 1)", id='f9b560e2-8f1f-46d8-a51d-2cd682590630', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b9299c9339f32cd5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8931c90d-d11e-428f-8866-1e5dd2b870f2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" microservice is failing. They already tried getting traces with get_traces, but it returned an empty list. That means there\'s no trace data available for the profile service in the last 5 minutes. Hmm, maybe the service isn\'t generating traces, or the issue isn\'t captured in the traces.\n\nNext, they should check the dependency graph to see if the profile service is interacting with other services that might be causing the problem. If the dependency graph shows that the profile service is dependent on other services like MongoDB or Memcached, maybe those services are having issues. But the user mentioned that the endpoints for those services were present, so maybe not. Wait, but the dependency graph could show if there are any unexpected connections or if the profile service is not communicating properly with its dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics with get_metrics could help. They can look for errors, latency, or other anomalies in the profile service\'s metrics. For example, if there\'s a high number of error counters or increased latency, that could indicate a problem. The user mentioned that CPU and memory metrics were normal, but maybe there\'s something else in the metrics that\'s not obvious.\n\nAnother possibility is checking the logs directly using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to look for any error messages in the pod\'s logs. Since the exit code was 2, which is a generic error, the logs might have more details. But the user previously checked the logs and didn\'t find any obvious errors. Maybe they need to check the logs again with a different approach, like tailing the logs in real-time or looking for specific error patterns.\n\nWait, the user also mentioned that the container image might be faulty. So maybe they should check the image\'s integrity or try rolling back to a previous version. But how? They might need to check the image\'s tags or verify if the image was pulled correctly. The exec_kubectl_cmd_safely could be used to run a command inside the pod to check the image version or run a health check.\n\nAlternatively, checking the deployment configuration to see if there are any misconfigurations. For example, the deployment might be using the wrong image tag, or the environment variables are incorrect. Using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to describe the deployment or pod could reveal such issues.\n\nBut since the user is in the thinking stage, they need to choose the next tool. Since get_traces didn\'t return anything, maybe the next step is to check the dependency graph to see if there are any issues with the services the profile depends on. If the dependency graph shows that the profile service is not connected to its dependencies, that could be a problem. Alternatively, checking the metrics for the profile service\'s endpoints to see if there are any errors or high latency.\n\nWait, the user already checked the endpoints and found them present. So maybe the issue is within the profile service itself. The next step could be to check the logs again, but since the previous check didn\'t find errors, maybe they need to look for more detailed logs or check the container\'s exit code more thoroughly. Alternatively, they could try restarting the pod to see if the issue persists, but that\'s more of a mitigation step.\n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics to check for any anomalies in the profile service\'s metrics. For example, if there\'s a spike in error rates or a drop in request counts, that could indicate a problem. The query could be something like "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"profile\\"} [5m]))" to check the number of requests, or "count by (status_code) (sum over time (http_requests_total{job=\\"profile\\"} [5m]))" to see error rates.\n\nBut the user hasn\'t checked metrics yet. So the next logical step is to use get_metrics to gather more data on the profile service\'s performance and look for any signs of issues. That would help confirm if there\'s a problem with the service\'s operation, even if the traces are empty.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \n**Reasoning**: Since `get_traces` returned no data, the next step is to check real-time metrics for the "profile" service to identify anomalies like errors, latency spikes, or resource exhaustion. This will help determine if the service is crashing, timing out, or encountering unhandled exceptions.  \n\n**Args**:  \n- `query`: `sum(rate(profile_service_errors_total[5m]))` (to check error rates) and `avg(profile_service_latency_seconds)[5m]` (to check latency).', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 939, 'prompt_tokens': 2429, 'total_tokens': 3368, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0b-867a-7653-82c2-9bebe8002b4d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2429, 'output_tokens': 939, 'total_tokens': 3368}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9fe9c402-8d6a-4440-b359-e26f067bdcbd'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" microservice is failing. They already checked the traces and got an empty response, so no traces found. Now they need to check metrics.\n\nThe next logical step is to look at metrics that could indicate issues. The user mentioned using get_metrics with specific PromQL queries. The first query should check for error rates, maybe something like sum(rate(profile_service_errors_total[5m])) to see if there are any errors being reported. Then, checking latency with avg(profile_service_latency_seconds)[5m] to see if the service is slow. \n\nWait, but the user might not know the exact metric names. However, based on common Prometheus metrics, "errors_total" and "latency_seconds" are typical. So, the tool call should include both queries to cover possible issues. The user needs to input these queries into get_metrics. \n\nSo, the tool call would be to get_metrics with the query parameter set to both PromQL expressions. That way, they can check for errors and latency spikes. If the metrics show high errors or high latency, that points to the problem. If not, maybe the issue is elsewhere, like the container image as previously thought.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum(rate(profile_service_errors_total[5m])) or avg(profile_service_latency_seconds)[5m]"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae6d2561e1fab9a0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 296, 'prompt_tokens': 3562, 'total_tokens': 3858, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0b-b1b1-7cf2-bf09-fcc35ab217fa-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum(rate(profile_service_errors_total[5m])) or avg(profile_service_latency_seconds)[5m]'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae6d2561e1fab9a0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3562, 'output_tokens': 296, 'total_tokens': 3858}), ToolMessage(content="('[prom_mcp] Error querying get_metrics: 400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: http://prometheus-server.observe.svc.cluster.local:80/api/v1/query?query=sum%28rate%28profile_service_errors_total%5B5m%5D%29%29+or+avg%28profile_service_latency_seconds%29%5B5m%5D', 80)", id='8fd3e1be-36f2-441e-b349-4fe9a44864c0', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ae6d2561e1fab9a0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c1e4ab1a-bb42-4076-badb-008ca852c2c1')]]
2026-03-17 09:06:27 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 2] Inject tool call prompt (repeated) - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_prompt_inject_step:85
2026-03-17 09:06:30 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 2] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b93cc0c4-67be-4a33-b490-c7d66f2d78ac'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application that has multiple microservices. They\'ve been using various tools to diagnose issues, and the main problem seems to be with the "profile" microservice.\n\nFirst, they checked the deployments and pods, and everything looked okay at first glance. But then they noticed some restarts in the pods, especially the "profile" pod. They looked into the logs, but there weren\'t any obvious errors. The CPU and memory metrics were normal, so resource issues weren\'t the problem. The dependency graph didn\'t show the profile service interacting with others, and the traces for the profile service were empty. \n\nThen, they checked the endpoints for the services the profile depends on, like MongoDB and Memcached, and those were present. When they described the pod, they saw that the container exited with code 2, which is a generic error. The image used was "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest", and it was pulled successfully. \n\nSince there were no specific errors in the logs or metrics, the next step was to consider the container image itself. Exit code 2 can mean different things, but without more info, it\'s hard to tell. The user concluded that the image might be misconfigured or incompatible, leading to silent failures. \n\nSo, the final answer points to the container image as the root cause. The user submitted that the issue is with the image, suggesting it needs re-validation or rebuilding. That makes sense because if the image is broken, the container would fail to start properly, leading to restarts without clear logs. The tools used didn\'t catch the image issue because the logs were silent, so the only clue was the exit code and the restarts.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure in the **"profile" microservice** is an issue with the container image **"yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest"**. The pod restarts with **exit code 2**, indicating an application-level error, but no specific logs or metrics reveal the exact cause. This suggests the image may be **misconfigured**, **missing dependencies**, or **incompatible with the runtime environment**, leading to silent failures. The image should be **re-validated or rebuilt** to resolve the issue. \n\n**Fault Location**:  \n- **Microservice**: `profile`  \n- **Root Cause**: Container image (`yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`) is faulty, causing silent application crashes and restarts.  \n\n**Recommendation**:  \nRebuild or re-validate the container image to ensure compatibility and correctness. Check for missing dependencies or configuration errors in the image.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0f5516ef-d635-4d80-aef9-1624b446a7c9'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b169707f-5afb-4724-a95c-26531dc96fb0'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where the "profile" microservice is having issues. The previous analysis pointed to the container image being faulty, causing silent failures. But I need to make sure that\'s the actual root cause.\n\nFirst, I should check if there are any traces or logs that might give more insight. The get_traces tool can help here. By looking at the Jaeger traces for the "profile" service, I can see if there are any errors or anomalies in the service\'s operations. If the traces are empty, that might confirm that the service isn\'t processing requests, which aligns with the exit code 2 issue.\n\nAlternatively, checking the dependency graph with get_dependency_graph could show if the profile service is interacting with other services incorrectly. But since the user mentioned that the dependency graph didn\'t show interactions, maybe that\'s not the issue. \n\nAnother angle is to check the metrics using get_metrics. If the profile service\'s metrics are abnormal, like high error rates or latency, that could indicate a problem. But since the user already checked CPU and memory metrics and they were normal, maybe that\'s not it. \n\nThe get_logs tool isn\'t listed, but the user mentioned logs didn\'t show errors. However, the exit code 2 is a generic error, so maybe the logs are not detailed enough. \n\nSince the user is considering the container image as the root cause, the next step is to verify if the image is indeed the problem. But without more data, it\'s hard to confirm. The get_traces tool can provide more detailed information about the service\'s operations, which might reveal if there\'s an error in the service\'s processing that\'s not captured elsewhere. \n\nSo, using get_traces for the "profile" service in the last 5 minutes could help identify if there are any traces of errors or if the service is failing silently. If there are no traces, it might confirm that the service isn\'t processing requests, pointing to the image issue. If there are traces, they might show the exact point of failure. \n\nTherefore, the next tool to use is get_traces with the service name "profile" and a reasonable time frame like 5 minutes.\n</think>\n\nget_traces  \n**Reasoning**: To investigate the "profile" microservice\'s behavior, we need to check if there are any traces of errors or anomalies in its operations. The exit code 2 suggests a silent failure, and traces can reveal if the service is crashing, timing out, or encountering unhandled exceptions. This will help confirm if the issue is within the service\'s logic or external dependencies.  \n\n**Args**:  \n- `service`: "profile"  \n- `last_n_minutes`: 5', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 563, 'prompt_tokens': 2232, 'total_tokens': 2795, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0b-5f5c-79e3-ba94-397a7b3eb539-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2232, 'output_tokens': 563, 'total_tokens': 2795}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9941c0de-7946-47c7-8c20-751077758f30'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me think. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" microservice is failing. They mentioned that the pod restarts with exit code 2, but there are no obvious errors in the logs or metrics. The previous step was to call get_traces for the "profile" service over the last 5 minutes. \n\nWait, the tool call needs to be in the correct JSON format. The function is get_traces, and the arguments are service and last_n_minutes. The service name is "profile", and the time frame is 5 minutes. So the tool call should have the name as "get_traces" and the arguments as {"service": "profile", "last_n_minutes": 5}. \n\nI need to make sure that the JSON is properly formatted, with the service name as a string and the minutes as an integer. Also, check if the parameters are required. The get_traces function requires both service and last_n_minutes, which are provided. Alright, that should be correct.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "profile", "last_n_minutes": 5}', 'name': 'get_traces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b9299c9339f32cd5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, 