e64-12077f8f8828'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" microservice in the hotel reservation application is having issues. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and the profile pod is running but has restarted twice. The logs from the pod don\'t show any obvious errors, just some informational messages. The exit code was 2, which is generic, so they need to dig deeper.\n\nHmm, the next step is to check the container image. The logs don\'t have specific errors, so maybe the image is the problem. The user mentioned the image is "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest". Maybe the image is misconfigured or there\'s a compatibility issue. They should check the image\'s health and ensure it\'s correctly built. \n\nWait, the user has the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool. They can try to describe the pod to get more details about the container, like the image version or any startup issues. Alternatively, they could check the image\'s pull status or see if there are any events related to the pod\'s restarts. \n\nAnother angle: since the pod is restarting, maybe there\'s a liveness probe failure. The user should check the pod\'s status and any events related to crashes. Using kubectl describe pod might show why the container is exiting. But the user can\'t use interactive commands, so they need to use the exec tool to run commands like describe or get events. \n\nWait, the user already tried logs and didn\'t find errors. The next logical step is to check the pod\'s status and events. So, using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run "kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation" would give details on why the pod is restarting. That could show if there\'s a crash loop, resource issues, or image pull errors. \n\nAlternatively, checking the container\'s image pull status. If the image is pulling correctly, maybe there\'s a runtime issue. But since the logs show the image was pulled successfully, maybe the problem is within the application code. \n\nSo, the best next step is to describe the pod to get detailed information about its status, events, and any errors that might not be in the logs. That should help pinpoint the root cause.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \n**Reasoning**: The logs show no explicit errors, but the pod restarted twice. To diagnose the root cause, we need to inspect the pod\'s status, events, and container details (e.g., image pull status, liveness probes, or resource constraints). This will clarify why the container exited with code 2.  \n\n**Args**:  \n- `command`: `kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation`', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 581, 'prompt_tokens': 6582, 'total_tokens': 7163, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0c-9a8b-7602-808c-5f06dccd1fef-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6582, 'output_tokens': 581, 'total_tokens': 7163}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7bbdb590-a1f1-4472-9ffa-23eed38068f5'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the "profile" pod is restarting. They checked the logs and didn\'t see any errors, but the pod has restarted twice. The next step is to get more details about the pod\'s status and events.\n\nThe command they want to run is `kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation`. This command will provide detailed information about the pod, including its status, events, and any issues that might have caused it to restart. \n\nBut wait, the tool they have is `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely`, which can only handle one command at a time. The previous attempt to use a pipe failed, so they need to make sure the command is simple. The `describe` command is a single command and shouldn\'t have any issues. \n\nThey should call `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` with the `describe` command to get the pod\'s details. This will help identify if there\'s a problem with the container image, resource limits, or some other issue causing the restarts. \n\nSo the correct tool call is to use `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` with the `describe` command for the specific pod in the namespace. That should give the necessary information to pinpoint the root cause.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b4f66d92e6ab8ae1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 322, 'prompt_tokens': 7712, 'total_tokens': 8034, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb0c-b704-7922-94ae-1bcb802872b0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b4f66d92e6ab8ae1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7712, 'output_tokens': 322, 'total_tokens': 8034}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             profile-69cb98cb67-rtsbd\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.32\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 09:01:32 +0000\nLabels:           io.kompose.service=profile\n                  pod-template-hash=69cb98cb67\nAnnotati