ls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3174, 'output_tokens': 266, 'total_tokens': 3440}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2a5da1cb-3d8f-4ed3-bd97-4cdfea077dcf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out the next step. The user is trying to check if the "profile" service exists in the hotel-reservation namespace. They tried using `kubectl get deployments,pods` but got an error because pipe commands are forbidden. So, they need to split the command into two separate kubectl commands.\n\nFirst, they should check the deployments. If the "profile" deployment isn\'t there, that\'s a problem. Then, check the pods to see if any related to "profile" are running. The user needs to run two separate commands: one for deployments and one for pods. \n\nWait, the tool allows only one command at a time. So, the user should first run `kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation` to check deployments. If that doesn\'t show "profile", then run `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` to check pods. But since the user can only make one tool call at a time, they need to do this in two steps. \n\nIn the previous attempt, the user tried to split the commands but the tool rejected it. So, the correct approach is to first check deployments, then pods. Let me structure the tool calls accordingly. First, get deployments, then if needed, get pods. That way, they can identify if the deployment or pods are missing.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a574b8e11ec5b8c1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 306, 'prompt_tokens': 4295, 'total_tokens': 4601, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 